\taxon{Remark}
\title{Alternate theory of a directed interval}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\import{common}

\p{
  There's an alternative weaker presentation of the theory of a directed interval as that of bounded distributive lattices, given by replacing the total relation #{\leq}
by two function symbols #{{\lor}, {\land} \colon \directed-interval \times \directed-interval \to \directed-interval}
   satisfying the laws

  \ul{
    \li{#{t \lor t \jdgeq t}}
    \li{#{t \lor s \jdgeq s \lor t}}
    \li{#{t \lor (s \lor u) \jdgeq (t \lor s) \lor u}}
    \li{#{t \lor 0 \jdgeq t}}
    \li{#{t \land t \jdgeq t}}
    \li{#{t \land s \jdgeq s \land t}}
    \li{#{t \land (s \land u) \jdgeq (t \land s) \land u}}
    \li{#{t \land 1 \jdgeq t}}
    \li{#{t \land (t \lor s) \jdgeq t}}
    \li{#{t \lor (t \land s) \jdgeq t}}
    \li{#{t \land (s \lor u) \jdgeq (t \land s) \lor (t \land u)}.}
  }

  This theory relates to the [CCHM cubical interval](CCHM15), which
  features the above together with a DeMorgan involution.
}

\p{
  When interpreting this theory in Martin–Löf type theory, one must assume as an axiom that #{\directed-interval} forms a set.
}
